NAME
       fi_mon_sampler - Simple sampler for ofi_hook_monitor provider.

SYNOPSIS
               fi_mon_sampler [OPTIONS] <target>      sample from file(s) at <target>

DESCRIPTION
       Extract  data  from  the  ofi_hook_monitor  provider via communication
       files.  <target> can either be one communication file or a  folder  of
       files.   Data  is  exported based on -f <format> and either printed to
       stdout (only for single files), or stored per communication file at -o
       <outpath>.  The sampler can watch the communication files for  changes
       via the option -w <msec> for repeated sampling.

       The  name  format of the output files is based on the ofi_hook_monitor
       provider  and  is  as   follows:   <ppid>_<pid>_<sequential   id>_<job
       id>_<provider  name>.   ppid and pid are taken from the perspective of
       the monitored application.  In a batched  environment  running  SLURM,
       job id is set to the SLURM job ID, otherwise it is set to 0.

HOW TO RUN
       Launch  a  libfabric  application  with  FI_HOOK=monitor to enable the
       ofi_hook_monitor provider.  Adjust the monitor provider  settings  ac‐
       cording to fi_hook(7).

       Then  launch  the sampler via fi_mon_sampler -o <output> <target>.  By
       default,   the    ofi_hook_monitor    provider    stores    data    at
       /dev/shm/ofi/<uid>/<hostname>.

       The  sampler  will generate output files in the directory specified at
       <output>, one for each monitored provider.

OPTIONS
       -w <msec>
              Watch files for changes, check every <msec> milliseconds.

       -f <format>
              Output format.  Currently only supports CSV.

       -o <outpath>
              Output file path.

       -s     Print output to stdout.  Only works if  <target>  is  a  single
              file.

       -r <remote URL>
              URL of remote output database.  Enables monitoring data pusher.
              Specified database endpoint must accept format specified in -f.

       -m <KiB>
              Size of pusher data buffer in KiB.

       -l     Enable  performance  logging of sampler.  See Environment Vari‐
              ables for output options.

       -v     Enable verbose output.

USAGE EXAMPLES
       Launch  a  libfabric  application  and  enable  the   ofi_hook_monitor
       provider:

              FI_HOOK=monitor fi_pingpong [OPTIONS]

       Launch another fi_pingpong with the respective settings.

       Finally, launch the sampler:

              fi_mon_sampler -o $HOME -w 1000 -f csv /dev/shm/ofi/$UID/$HOSTNAME

OUTPUT
       Output files will be generated in the folder specified at -o <output>.

   Entries
       The  sampler  reports  count and sum counters per bucket for all moni‐
       tored libfabric functions.  Refer to fi_hook(7) for more details.

       In addition, several metadata fields are reported:

       • hostname: hostname as reported by gethostname(2)

       • job_id: value of SLURM_JOB_ID if present, otherwise 0

       • {p}pid: (parent) process ID

       • uid: UID of monitored application

       • provider: name of monitored provider

       • provider_id: ID of provider

       • child_provider_ids: comma-separated list of child IDs for  aggregat‐
         ing providers such as lnx or rx{d,m}

       • context_type: one of endpoint, domain, completion-queue

       • context_id: ID of context

   Formats
   CSV
       In  -f  csv mode, this will contain a CSV file with data for all moni‐
       tored libfabric functions.  For each function, both the count and  sum
       counters  are  exported, indicated by the column name suffix _c and _s
       respectively.  In addition, each function is monitored for  each  data
       size bucket.

       Example CSV output, first four columns, first three rows:

              mon_recv_0_64_c,mon_recv_0_64_s,mon_recv_64_512_c,mon_recv_64_512_s
              0,0,0,0
              22529,0,0,0
              113664,0,0,0

   Influx
       In  -f influx mode, the Influx Line Protocol is used.  The measurement
       name is ofi_hook_monitor.  Example influx output:

              ofi_hook_monitor,function=mon_recv,
              bucket=0_64,pid=1287116,ppid=1284798,
              monitor_id=1,job_id=0 count=32,sum=0 1763393626427182336

   Remote Output
       The option -r can be used to set output to a  remote  database.   This
       database  must accept the format specified in -f.  Many databases sup‐
       port the Influx Line protocol.  Should a database require  authentica‐
       tion,  then  you  can  use  the MON_SAMPLER_REMOTE_HEADERS environment
       variable (see Environment Variables).

Environment Variables
       MON_SAMPLER_REMOTE_HEADERS
              Headers as string for remote output pusher (default: ’’,  exam‐
              ple: Authorization: Token <token>).

       MON_SAMPLER_PERF_LOG_PUSHER_OUTPATH
              Path  to  output  file  for  sampler  log  (default: ./log_sam‐
              pler.csv)

       MON_SAMPLER_PERF_LOG_SAMPLER_OUTPATH
              Path to output file for pusher log (default: ./log_pusher.csv)

       MON_SAMPLER_SPEED_LIMIT_KBS
              Maximum upload speed in KB/second.  (default: unlimited)

       MON_SAMPLER_PUSHER_MAX_WAIT_S
              Maximum wait time in seconds for pusher before pushing data  to
              database.  (default 5)

Sampler Performance Logging
       The  maximum  achievable frequency at which the monitoring sampler can
       sample depends on a variety of factors, such as num  number  of  moni‐
       tored  libfabric processes or the amount of processed monitoring data.
       If option -r is specified, then the sampler will  additionally  launch
       the  pusher component, which is a second thread asynchronously upload‐
       ing batches of monitored data to a remote database.  In order to eval‐
       uate which sampling frequency is achievable without  missing  sampling
       deadlines,  and in order to tune sampler and transmission system para‐
       meters, a simple performance log can be enabled by setting -b.

       In this mode, the sampler will open and  populate  two  CSV  files  at
       MON_SAMPLER_PERF_LOG_PUSHER_OUTPATH    and   MON_SAMPLER_PERF_LOG_SAM‐
       PLER_OUTPATH, which contain information on the performance of the sam‐
       pler and pusher respectively.  These CSV files have the following for‐
       mat:

       Sampler:

       • time: UNIX epoch timestamp of sample iteration

       • num_files: number of sampled files

       • sample_time_ms: sample time in milliseconds

       • serialized_elements: number of serialized elements

       • serialized_bytes: number of serialized bytes

       • procs_with_data: processes with data

       Pusher:

       • time: UNIX epoch timestamp of pusher run

       • buffer_size: size of transmit buffer in bytes

       • upload_time_ms: upload time in milliseconds

